    "id": "the-price-of-a-working-service",
    "title": "The lowest equipment quote: a bargain only after the missing costs",
    "summary": "Compare the same complete service, test the workload assumptions and decide whether the lower quote still offers better value.",
    "intro": "The lowest quote may be the best choice. First establish what it buys. Two proposals can name the same machine while leaving very different bills for probes, software, installation and support. Compare a complete required package, then look for simpler suitable options and test the assumptions that could change the winner.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Make the proposals describe the same purchase",
        "Suppose two suppliers offer an ultrasound system. One includes the required probes, cart, software and delivery; the other lists a console with accessories to be confirmed. The lower figure may remain lower once complete, but it has not yet earned that conclusion. Give both suppliers one written configuration and ask them to identify exclusions and substitutions. Keep unknown costs unresolved until there is a defensible estimate. Entering zero converts missing information into an apparent saving."
      ],
      [
        "Put recurring commitments beside the purchase price",
        "For a laboratory service, WHO identifies reagent stability, shelf life and the intended setting as procurement considerations. That is a useful factual reminder that an instrument and its supply chain belong in the same decision. The broader purchasing method is straightforward: list the initial package, recurring consumables, maintenance, licenses and likely replacement items over an agreed period. Use the facility’s own volumes and assumptions. Show low and high scenarios for uncertain items instead of presenting a precise total built from guesses."
      ],
      [
        "Price the response the hospital would actually make",
        "An outage can mean using another room, sending samples elsewhere, transferring work or postponing a service. Ask the department which alternative is practical, for how long and at what identifiable cost. Avoid assigning a precise daily loss when the evidence cannot support it. A promise to answer a call is different from attendance, diagnosis, parts delivery and restoration. Record those milestones separately, and confirm the terms of any proposed loan unit. This makes the disruption comparison useful without inventing a failure rate."
      ],
      [
        "Do not turn total cost into an excuse to overbuy",
        "A larger package is not automatically better. Remove features that the clinical team does not need, compare simpler suitable options and question optimistic workload projections. A sophisticated system can carry costs that do not produce useful capacity in the receiving facility. The aim is a complete minimum specification with room for justified growth, not the most elaborate proposal. Record who approved each essential requirement so the comparison can survive changes in staff or supplier."
      ],
      [
        "Find the assumption that changes the decision",
        "Find the single uncertain cost most likely to change the choice. Suppose two otherwise suitable, complete offers differ by US$2,000. The cheaper one excludes an interface whose actual price is unknown. Resolve that interface before negotiating a small discount on freight: any interface charge above US$2,000 reverses the initial-price ranking. This is a hypothetical threshold, not a market quote or a prediction.\n\nThen test lower use over the same planning period. A supply pack with 100 tests is not worth 100 completed results if only 40 can be used before expiry; include quality-control use and repeats too. Keep the distinction between cash paid, stock still usable and stock discarded. Choose the lower complete cost when the capabilities and obligations meet the brief; spend more only for a benefit the department can name and use."
      ]
    ],
