    "id": "service-starts-before-delivery",
    "title": "Medical equipment service contracts: buy a route back to work",
    "summary": "Compare maintenance, repair coverage and restoration responsibilities against the disruption your department can manage.",
    "intro": "A prompt answer is useful, but the department also needs to know how diagnosis, parts, attendance and return to service will happen. Settle those responsibilities while the purchase is negotiable. Compare the written coverage with the facility’s repair capacity, backup equipment and ability to absorb an excluded bill.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Separate the services inside the promise",
        "Technical advice, planned maintenance, fault diagnosis, repair, parts and training are different commitments. State who provides each, where it happens and what is excluded. Include engineer travel, freight and major accessories such as batteries or probes. Ask how a fault crossing software, an accessory and the main device will be coordinated. A usable agreement makes the responsible provider visible instead of leaving the hospital to resolve the boundary during an outage."
      ],
      [
        "Define each repair milestone",
        "Separate acknowledgement, attendance, diagnosis, parts delivery and restoration. Record the hours and locations covered, the information needed to start the process and what stops or changes a promised timetable. If the department relies on a loan unit, establish the configuration, delivery arrangements and availability conditions. Have the clinical service describe the alternative it can use during an outage and how long that option is workable. This grounds the coverage decision in an actual continuity plan."
      ],
      [
        "Give the equipment a useful record",
        "At handover, collect the serial number, configuration, instructions, acceptance documentation, maintenance requirements and support contacts in the facility’s asset record. Keep accessory identities where they affect compatibility. Record service events so a future technician can understand what changed. The first fault report should not require reconstructing the purchase from scattered emails and photographs."
      ],
      [
        "Plan for safety notices and changes",
        "Manufacturers and regulators publish corrections and recalls that may affect particular models or serial ranges. FDA’s recall database is one public reference for U.S. information. Assign someone to receive applicable manufacturer notices and route them to the clinical and technical owners. A device being absent from one search does not establish that every update is complete. Preserve the evidence of any required corrective work with the unit’s records."
      ],
      [
        "Buy the coverage the department can use",
        "Compare broad coverage with planned maintenance plus separately billed repairs using the same labour, parts, travel and freight scope. In a hypothetical annual comparison, broad cover costs US$6,000 and planned maintenance costs US$2,000. The additional US$4,000 buys the specified transfer of repair costs and service commitments. It is not automatically a saving: test what one quoted excluded repair would cost under each option, and whether that repair is actually covered. Do not invent a failure probability to force a winner.\n\nSeparate billing can work where qualified support, reserve funds and acceptable cover are available. Broader coverage is more attractive where a large repair bill would be difficult to fund and the provider can deliver the promised local work. A contract that excludes the expensive probe, tube or detector may leave the main financial exposure unchanged. Keep the return-to-service checks and loaner terms explicit whichever route is chosen."
      ]
    ],
